  "image_name": "29ae13bc1ef7e126929b9da32c6ebe95.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares microbial habitats and chemical cycling in two icy environments: a glacier/ice sheet and sea ice. In both settings, microbes arrive partly through atmospheric deposition and then occupy distinct surface and subsurface niches shaped by light, meltwater, nutrients, and mineral particles.\n\nIn the glacier system, snow and ice surfaces host snow algae, ice algae, bacteria, cyanobacteria, and dust-rich cryoconite holes. Meltwater and cracks deliver cells and nutrients downward into englacial and subglacial habitats. At the glacier base, subglacial lakes, sediments, and bedrock minerals support redox reactions between oxidized and reduced compounds. Labels indicate exchanges involving iron, sulfur, nitrogen species, methane, and carbon dioxide, suggesting active chemolithoautotrophic and heterotrophic processes beneath the ice. Microorganisms and their products are then discharged in subglacial fluids to downstream waters.\n\nIn the sea-ice system, microbes inhabit snow, melt ponds, brine channels, frost flowers, and the underside of the ice, where algal mats are shown. The diagram emphasizes how sea-ice properties vary from multi-year to first-year ice and how temperature, nutrients, brine, insulation, and UV radiation influence these communities. Overall, the image shows ice as a layered, dynamic microbial ecosystem rather than a sterile environment.",
